From 883b5f1e923b8becbbbb5b0393ccea11c65baa7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: legsmechanical <joshgaine@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 22:11:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] feat(shadow): let a module claim Undo/Copy/Delete
 (claims_edit_ccs)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Undo (CC 56), Copy (CC 60) and Delete (CC 119) reach Move firmware and are not
forwarded to modules. A module that wants them for its own gestures — hold Copy
+ tap a pad to target it, tap Undo to revert — cannot have them: the press also
fires Move's own clip copy/undo/delete behind the screen.

PR #154 addressed this by blocking all three whenever the shadow display was up,
and was reverted in PR #175 because the block was unconditional: it stole Move's
native Undo during ordinary chain use, so a note edit could not be undone while
any module was on screen. That revert names the right shape — capability-gated
capture, where the module declares it claims the buttons. This implements it.

  "capabilities": { "claims_edit_ccs": true }

While that module's UI is on screen (hierarchy editor, component edit/params, or
a type:"canvas" UI, fullscreen or co-run overlay) the three CCs are withheld from
Move firmware and forwarded to the module. Every other module, and every other
screen, is unaffected — Move keeps its own buttons. Mute (CC 88) stays
unclaimable; Move-native Mute+Pad depends on it reaching firmware.

The claim is re-derived in ONE place — a tick-time reconcile against whatever is
on screen — rather than bookkept at the flag's write sites, so it cannot be left
set by a path that forgot to clear it. shadow_ui consults the module metadata
only when the on-screen identity actually changes (the lookup is a file read),
and the shim independently drops the claim when the shadow display closes, so a
shadow_ui that exits or crashes cannot strand it.

Press/release is latched per button, so whoever received the press also receives
the release even if the claim changes mid-hold. Without that, a claim engaging
between press and release leaves Move believing the button is still held, and a
claim dropping mid-hold hands Move an orphan release.

shadow_control_t.edit_cc_block is carved from the existing reserved8 padding, so
the struct size and every field offset are unchanged and a mismatched
shim/shadow_ui pair cannot arise from it.

Also corrects docs/MODULES.md, which still described PR #154's behaviour as
current: the code was reverted in July 2026 but the documentation was not, and it
claimed a firmware block that no longer existed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XX8gDJhHpYErLEwJsV5jPm
